 stem cells came  from an African clawed frog known by the  Latin name Xenopus laevis but these  living robots don't resemble any living  creature after being harvested the stem  cells were then separated into single  cells and left to incubate the cells  were cut and joined together under a  microscope the resulting Assembly of  living cells has been dubbed as Zeno  BOTS and they're tiny at just 0.04  inches or one millimeter wide these  robots can move independently and  collectively and survive for weeks at a  time  they could potentially be used to  transport medicines inside a patient's  body as well as other uses and don't  worry who'll get to all of this later on  in the episode but the thing about this  that amazed me the most was how it was  all designed the new creatures were  designed by a supercomputer at the  University of Vermont and then assembled  and tested by biologists at Tufts  University he is how it worked the deep  green supercomputer cluster at the  University of Vermont ran for months at  a time this computer was custom designed  allowing for cutting-edge AI research  four miles east of UVM central campus  now home to the university's new  supercomputer dubbed deep green intended  for research into machine learning and  artificial intelligence the machine said  to be among the 100 most powerful in the  nation it's about 800 to a thousand  normal laptops one of deep greens  graphic processing units removed for  demonstration was impressive enough we  have enemies across the entire cluster  it's almost half a million processing  cores  deep green exceeds petaflop speed one  thousand million million calculations  per second with its wide ranging  applications it is a very big deal this  will allow us to be fairly competitive  with much larger universities and allow  us to do research that we would not have  been able to do it used an evolutionary  algorithm to create thousands of  candidate designs for the new life-forms  the scientists determined a desired task  in this case the task was movement so  the algorithm would create designs  intended to produce the outcome of  movement  within its software the computer would  assemble a few hundred simulated heart  and skin cells into different body forms  the behavior of these cells was governed  by constraints and parameters for  example the maximum muscle power of a  frog heart cell would these parameters  as its guide the algorithm iterated the  life-form designs the more successful  simulated organisms were allowed to  reproduce inside the algorithm while the  failed designs were thrown away after a  hundred independent runs of the  algorithm the most promising designs  were selected for testing and then later  brought to life lead study author Sam  Krugman speaks quote eventually it was  able to give us the science that were  actually transferable to real cells that  was a breakthrough there's no external  control from a remote control or  bioelectricity this is an autonomous  agent it's almost like a wind-up toy as  these biological devices were assembled  into body forms never before seen in  nature the cells began to work together  the heart cells as the muscles  contracted work to provide an ordered  forward motion as simulated and  predicted by the computers design the  contractions of the heart muscles were  the mechanisms for motion the skin cells  on the other hand behaved as a  scaffolding to hold everything together  the organisms managed to survive in  their watery environment for weeks they  were powered by stored energy in the  form of proteins the Zener BOTS moved  objects around in the real world to when  their environment was scattered with  particles the Zener BOTS spontaneously  worked together moving in a circular  motion to push the particles into one  spot so what can these things be used  for Michael Levin co-leader of the study  and director of the center of  regenerative and developmental biology  speaks quote we can imagine many useful  applications of these living robots like  searching out nasty compounds or  radioactive contamination gathering  micro plastics in the oceans traveling  in arteries to scrape out plaque end  quote  the scientists also hint that these  machines could be used to carry medicine  to a specific area inside of a patient  the robots also have the benefit of  healing themselves after being cut quote  we sliced the robot almost in half and  it stitches itself back up and keeps  going and this is something that you  can't do with typical machines end quote  so this is quite significant because  today many technologies are made from  metals concrete or plastic and while  these are great for our needs they can  also create ecological and human health  problems like the growing problem of  plastic pollution in the ocean and the  toxicity of many synthetic materials in  electronics but according to the  scientists quote these xeno bots are  fully biodegradable when they're done  with their job after seven days they're  just dead skin cells end quote so with  all of this in mind people are obviously  going to be split on this issue blurring  the lines between robots and living  organisms our popular subjects and  science fiction many people worry about  the implications of a rapid  technological advancement in ever more  complex biological manipulations Michael  Levin of the study speaks quote when we  start to mess around with complex  systems that we don't understand we're  going to get unintended consequences  nevertheless building on simple organic  forms like the Senna BOTS could also  lead to beneficial discoveries he goes  on if Humanity is going to survive in  the future we need to better understand  how complex properties somehow emerge  from simple rules a first step towards  doing that is to explore how do living  systems decide what an overall behavior  should be and how do we manipulate the  species to get the behaviors we want  it's strange to think that if left  aligned to their natural development the  stem cells taken from the embryonic  frogs would have turned into skin and  heart tissue within living breathing  animals instead in configurations  designed by a computer algorithm they  were constructed by humans and those  cells were assembled into something  completely new a living programmable  robot although the current states of  Zeno BOTS is relatively harmless there  is the potential for future work to  incorporate a nervous system and  countless other applications as this  field of research grows regulations and  ethics guidelines will need to be  seriously considered this is an immense  responsibility for humans to have and  one of the biggest questions to if this  should be done or not is do the benefits  outweigh the risks  at this stage it's impossible to know  though I do think we should be cautious  in how we proceed biological unintended  consequences are no joke but then again  the correct use of such technology to  clean up our oceans or other such  applications could be a game changer for  those who want to read the study the  research has been published in the  Proceedings of the National Academy of  Sciences and the team has made their  source code freely available on github  I'll leave links for that below if you  want to see more amazing biological  breakthroughs definitely check out my  other videos on this topic so what do  you guys think should humans mess with  nature like this or is this a recipe for  disaster  in your mind do the benefits outweigh  the risks let me know in the comments  section below if you did enjoy this  video please consider subscribing and if  you are a subscriber but haven't checked  out my book new thinking yet it's been  rated the ninth best technology history  book of all time so it's definitely  worth a read to put our current world  into perspective anyway this has been to  gogo and you've been watching cold  fusion and I'll catch you again soon for  the  cheers guys  [Music]  Oh  [Music]  [Music]
 

